## The problem

In adolescent idiopathic scoliosis, doctors need to know how much each vertebra is rotated. On a standard X-ray this is usually judged by eye with the Nash–Moe grading system, which gives only rough categories.

## What we built

VerteRo is a fully automated pipeline that reads a standard posteroanterior X-ray and estimates each vertebra's rotation in degrees. It works in four stages: find the spine region, segment each vertebra, locate the pedicles, then calculate the rotation angle from their geometry. We compared three detection models (Faster R-CNN, YOLOv11 L and YOLOv12 L) for the pedicle step.

## Results

- Faster R-CNN detected pedicles best, with an F1 score of 0.93.
- Mean absolute error of 8.2° against reference angles.
- Exact agreement with Nash–Moe grading on 71% of vertebrae.

## Publication

Kotheeranurak V, Sunpaweravong S, **Pongyanpat K**, Thanasuttiwat J, Jaroenwareekul S, Panya-Amornwat T, Singhatanadgige W, Limthongkul W, Honsawek S, Lipikorn R. *VerteRo: A Fully Automated Deep Neural Network Tool for Estimating Axial Vertebral Body Rotation in Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis.* Global Spine Journal. 2026;16(8):3918–3926. [doi:10.1177/21925682261448826](https://doi.org/10.1177/21925682261448826)
